    "lawCode": "Amd §9-h, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "Banking Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7973A\n\nSPONSOR: MARCHIONE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the banking law, in relation to\nprohibiting certain check cashing fees\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the banking law, to prohibit any banking organiza-\ntion from imposing, or seeking to impose, any service charge, or from\ncollecting any fee, for the cashing of a check or draft drawn on an\naccount at such banking organization, from a person to whom such check\nor draft is payable, regardless of whether or not such person is a\ncustomer of such banking organization.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would amend section 9 -h of the banking law to:\n\n*Prohibit any banking organization from imposing, or seeking to impose,\nany service charge, or from collecting any fee, for the cashing of a\ncheck or draft drawn on an account at such banking organization, from a\nperson to whom such check or draft is payable, regardless of whether or\nnot such person is a customer of such banking organization;\n\n*Expressly provide that nothing in this bill would:\n\no Require a banking organization to cash a check or a draft where the\naccount from which such check or draft is payable does not maintain\nsufficient funds to pay such check or draft;\no Prohibit any banking organization from charging any account holder any\nservice charge for the cashing of a check or a draft; and/or\no Be deemed to prevent any banking organization from requesting to exam-\nine from any person seeking to cash a check or a draft drawn on such\nbanking organization, for sufficient identification necessary to proper-\nly identify such person as the person to whom the check or draft is\npayable, and that for purposes of this bill, a valid and up to date\ndriver's license, non-driver's identification card, or United States\npassport shall be deemed to constitute sufficient identification.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn a period spanning roughly the last decade, banks have devised various\nfees, of which some are intended by the banks to improve the in-building\nexperience of their own customers. The most notable example of this is\nthe imposition of a fee on non-customers who are cashing checks at a\nbank, even if the check in question is being drawn from an account at\nthat bank. According to the Los Angeles Times in 2017, Bank of America\nis among the most recent of banks to increase their fee on non-customers\nto eight dollars per transaction.\n\nIt has been the policy of this State through the banking law to limit,\nwhere possible, excessive fees for taking part in simple financial tran-\nsactions. It does not follow, therefore, that an individual who attempts\nto draw on an account at a bank for which they have a check should be\ncharged a fee for doing so, even if they are not a customer. This is\nespecially the case since the bank's customer themselves wrote the check\nto such person. Accordingly, not only does this create an unnecessary\nfinancial burden on the person cashing the check, but in some instances\nit essentially creates a principle of imposing two fees on the same\naccount if the person holding the account also pays an annual account\nfee.\n\nThis bill would make it so that banking organizations can no longer\nimpose a non-customer check cashing fee on checks being drawn from an\naccount at that particular bank, thereby ensuring the State's policy\nagainst excessive fees be maintained.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone noted.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
